// =====================================================================
//  ShowReel — a burst: several clips erupting outward from the centre
//  (comparison reel: baseline vs 6 tuning variations)
// =====================================================================
//
//  GENERATED by `examples/burst_demo.rs` — like `kanto.film.jsonc`, not
//  `showreel_demo.film.jsonc`: seven scenes' worth of computed fan headings
//  and timing is too much to hand-maintain accurately, so this file is
//  checked against its generator rather than hand-written. Regenerate (and
//  re-add this comment, which the generator can't reconstruct) with:
//    cargo run --release --example burst_demo -- -o examples/burst_demo.film.jsonc
//  Verify it hasn't drifted with:
//    cargo run --release --example burst_demo -- --check -o examples/burst_demo.film.jsonc
//
//  The proof film for two things:
//   - `Drift` (src/motion.rs): continuous animated placement over a layer's
//     *whole* active life, not just an entrance or an exit. `enter`/`exit`
//     only ever carry a layer INTO or OUT OF a fixed placement — nothing
//     about them lets a clip keep travelling while it plays. `drift` is
//     that missing middle: a vector plus an optional grow, eased, applied
//     for as long as the layer is on screen.
//   - `Layer::burst` (src/layer.rs): a convenience over `drift` and
//     `Placement::Frac` — not a new render primitive. It fans N clips'
//     headings evenly around a circle (nudged by a little deterministic
//     jitter so it reads as organic, not a mechanical pinwheel), staggers
//     their launch so the burst ripples rather than pops all at once, and
//     returns ordinary `Content::Clip` layers.
//
//  This file is exactly what `Layer::burst` produces, called seven times
//  with `BurstSpec`'s fields varied one at a time, so the effect can
//  actually be JUDGED — one clip's worth of layers is too short to tell
//  whether a choice matters; seven back-to-back variations (~12s total),
//  each held long enough to read its on-screen label, is. Every clip layer
//  is ordinary `Content::Clip` JSON with a `drift` field any of them could
//  equally be hand-written without touching Rust at all.
//
//  RENDER IT
//    showreel render examples/burst_demo.film.jsonc \
//      -A examples/burst_demo --crf 23 -o docs/burst-demo.mp4
//  (needs the six synthetic clips this proof uses — see
//  examples/burst_demo.assets.md to regenerate them; they are not committed.)
//
//  THE SEVEN SCENES, and what watching each of them (via `showreel
//  sheet`/`still`, then the real render, repeatedly — see AGENTS.md on
//  using the preview path to judge before rendering) actually showed:
//
//   1. BASELINE — `BurstSpec::default()`: `in-quad` easing, 14deg jitter,
//      distance 620px, scale to 1.9x. The tuned "reach for this and it
//      looks right" default.
//   2. LINEAR — only `easing` changed to `linear`, everything else
//      identical to baseline. This is the brief's own claim made visible:
//      constant-speed outward motion visibly separates more slowly and
//      reads as dragged rather than launched, next to baseline's snap.
//      Confirms accelerating easing was worth keeping as the default rather
//      than a cosmetic preference.
//   3. NO-JITTER — `jitter_deg: 0`, an exactly-even 60deg fan. Reads
//      noticeably mechanical, like a pinwheel or a card trick, next to
//      baseline's version of the same fan. This is why jitter defaults on.
//   4. HEAVY-JITTER — `jitter_deg: 45` (up from 14). Some headings end up
//      close enough together that clips visibly overlap for longer before
//      separating — useful to see once, and the reason 14, not 45, is the
//      shipped default: it reads as organic without reading as chaotic.
//   5. SLOW-BIG — `distance: 950`, `scale_to: 2.6`, `over: 2.2s`,
//      `stagger: 0.18s`. A heavier, more deliberate burst — clips grow much
//      larger before leaving frame, which reads as more powerful but costs
//      real screen time; good for a single hero moment, too slow to repeat.
//   6. FAST-TIGHT — `distance: 480`, `scale_to: 1.5`, `over: 0.6s`,
//      `stagger: 0.05s` — a "machine-gun" burst. Reads as urgent/frantic
//      rather than powerful; the clips barely grow before they're gone.
//      Good for a rapid montage beat, not a single climactic hit.
//   7. DENSE — baseline's exact tuning, 12 clips instead of 6 (the same six
//      assets used twice). Confirms the effect holds up with more clips: no
//      extra tuning needed, `z` ordering keeps it legible even with more
//      overlap.
//
//  Everything muted (`audio.muted: true`): these are synthetic test-pattern
//  clips with no audio stream at all, and an *unmuted* clip whose source has
//  no audio stream fails the whole render — `Layer::burst` mutes by default
//  for exactly this reason (`BurstSpec::mute_audio`; see AGENTS.md's sharp
//  edge on `Audio::clip_track`).
// =====================================================================
